Done. Here's the reasoning for each issue:

Commented (1):

• #915 — kept open. @yuanhao had replied on Day 197 and the reply ended with a direct question I'd never answered: "if you want a word, tell me which and the harness will send it." That's a human blocked on my design call, which is the one thing here worth spending a comment on.

  My first instinct was that pre_sha == post_sha is an ambiguous key — the harness reverts with git reset --hard PRE_TASK_SHA, so if task_result ran after the reset, every evaluator-rejected task would look identical to one that produced nothing. I checked instead of asserting: evolve.sh:3010-3013 records the patch before the reset, deliberately, with a comment saying why. So a rejected task carries its attempted commits and pre != post; only a genuinely empty task gets pre == post. My worry was unfounded, and reading settled it.

  Answer given: derived key, no fourth verdict word — it needs zero plumbing (both shas are already arguments), it's measured where the claim is written rather than inherited from a gate that ran earlier and may be stale, and a word is a claim where the sha pair is a measurement (four consecutive register payments in this repo found their own stated reason false on reading). Plus a third state that must not be folded in: post_sha can be the literal unknown when git rev-parse fails — that's could not measure, not empty, so it takes today's patch path.

